{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "שתי נשים האחת אהובה והאחת שנואה", :translit "shtei nashim ha'achat ahuva veha'achat senu'a", :gloss "two wives, one beloved and one hated"} {:he "מרבה נשים מרבה כשפים", :translit "marbeh nashim marbeh keshafim", :gloss "multiplies wives, multiplies sorceries"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Moral observation: having two wives leads one to practice sorcery against the other"}], :frame {:text "The proverbial phrase 'one who multiplies wives multiplies sorceries' reflects a tradition that plurality of wives breeds strife and supernatural rivalry. The Sanhedrin (21a) cautions that a king 'shall not multiply wives' lest they turn away his heart.", :sources ["Sanhedrin 21a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "והיה הבן הבכור לשניאה", :translit "vehayah haben habechor lasenu'a", :gloss "and the firstborn son shall be of the hated one"} {:he "בני ט' מדות שמנו בנדרים", :translit "bnei tish'a midot shemanu benedarim", :gloss "nine categories enumerated in Nedarim"} {:he "בן סורר ומורה", :translit "ben sorer umoreh", :gloss "rebellious and defiant son"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition (semikhut): the section of the hated wife's son is adjacent to the rebellious son, implying cause and effect"}], :frame {:text "Mishnah Nedarim (9:10) lists nine categories of persons who can be the subject of vows, and the Talmud (Nedarim 9:10) derives lessons from this enumeration. The juxtaposition of the hated wife's firstborn with the rebellious son passage is read as signaling that the son of the hated wife is destined to become rebellious.", :sources ["Mishnah Nedarim 9:10" "Nedarim 9:10"]}} {:quotes [{:he "לשניאה", :translit "lasenu'a", :gloss "to the hated one"} {:he "לנשאיה", :translit "laneso'eha", :gloss "to her father-in-law"} {:he "שנואה בנשואיה", :translit "senu'a benesu'eha", :gloss "hated in her marriages"} {:he "במדרש דורש לאיש זה הקב\"ה", :translit "bamidrash dorash le'ish zeh hakadosh baruch hu", :gloss "in the Midrash it interprets 'for a man' — this is the Holy One blessed be He"} {:he "אהובה אלו העכו\"ם שמראה להם פנים", :translit "ahuva elu ha'akum shearay lahem panim", :gloss "beloved — these are the idolaters, to whom He shows His face"} {:he "שנואה אלו ישראל שמסתיר מהם פנים", :translit "senu'a elu yisrael shemastir mehem panim", :gloss "hated — these are Israel, from whom He hides His face"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "לשניאה", :b "לנשאיה"} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: שנואה (hated) shares root with נשואיה (her marriages) — hated in her marriages"} {:kind :other, :note "Midrashic reading: 'for a man' refers to God; the beloved are the nations, the hated are Israel"}], :frame {:text "The midrashic reading that 'for a man' (Deuteronomy 21:15) refers to the Holy One Blessed Be He is found in the Talmud (Chullin 60b), where God is identified through the term 'man' in this very verse. The motif that God shows His face to the nations while hiding it from Israel appears in contexts of divine providence (Deuteronomy 31:17–18).", :sources ["Chullin 60b" "Deuteronomy 31:17–18"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Moral observation: having two wives leads one to practice sorcery against the other", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Juxtaposition (semikhut): the section of the hated wife's son is adjacent to the rebellious son, implying cause and effect", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "לשניאה", :a-value 396, :b "לנשאיה", :b-value 396, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: שנואה (hated) shares root with נשואיה (her marriages) — hated in her marriages", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Midrashic reading: 'for a man' refers to God; the beloved are the nations, the hated are Israel", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:38:15.510947987Z", :model "glm-5.2"}